+def _held_on_line(wt: Path, branch: str, name: str, head: str, base_ref: str,
+                  paths: Sequence[str], took: Sequence[tuple[str, str]],
+                  beside: Mapping[str, str]) -> tuple[list[str], list[str]] | None:
+    """Whether bundle ``name``'s branch tip ``head``, which just failed to merge onto the line
+    checked out in ``wt`` (``paths``: the files it left conflicting) and which no same-wave
+    hold covers (:func:`_held_by`), is held for a clash with bundles already on the line
+    (#739) — and if so ``(partners, stranded)``. Decided by trial merges, never by which
+    branch touched a path:
+
+    * ``head`` must merge cleanly onto ``base_ref`` alone. Else its clash is with the base,
+      and None: the fold stops on it, as before.
+    * The bundles on the line are ``took`` (``(name, head)`` in merge order: merged by the
+      fold, or found on the line) after each of ``beside`` (name → head) the line carries —
+      the finished ids the flow's carry put on it (#646), which the fold does not list.
+    * They are judged with :func:`_clash` — the one classifier the carry uses (#694) — on
+      the commit where the line leaves ``base_ref`` (:func:`_cut_point`): the base each of
+      them was merged onto. So a bundle the line took from an older base than ``head``'s is
+      tried on the base it was cut from. ``"batch"``: the partners are the bundles whose own
+      change ``head`` conflicts with (:func:`_partners`). Any other kind: the ones whose
+      change ``head`` conflicts with only together with others of them, each one needed
+      for the clash (:func:`_together`). None when that names none: ``head`` merges after
+      them all (its clash is with something else the line took in, such as the base merged
+      in for a gone branch), one of them does not merge there, or no one of them is needed.
+    * ``stranded``: the partners whose own change does not merge cleanly onto ``base_ref``:
+      what the partner was built on (``under``, :func:`_built_on`) merges there, then its
+      head conflicts. The base moved since their branch was cut, and ``head``, which merges
+      onto the base, carries the change they clash with: the fix is theirs, not
+      ``name``'s. One whose branch fails there only for what it was built on — the line, or
+      another bundle's branch, stranded itself — is not: rebuilding it on the base fixes
+      nothing.
+
+    A trial or read whose git step fails gives None (the fold stops on the conflict); a
+    tree that will not go back raises. The trials never move the line (:func:`_trial`)."""
+    try:
+        if _trial(wt, branch, base_ref, [head])[0] is not None:
+            return None                          # a clash with the base itself
+        mine = dict(took)
+        on = [(n, h) for n, h in beside.items()
+              if n not in mine and n != name and _carries(wt, h, f"{n}'s head", branch)]
+        on += list(took)
+        if not on:
+            return None
+        root = _cut_point(wt, "HEAD", base_ref)[0] or base_ref
+        under = _built_on(wt, root, {**dict(on), name: head}, [n for n, _h in on])
+        kind, found, _files = _clash(wt, branch, head, root, paths, on, (), under)
+        if kind == "base":
+            return None
+        partners = (list(found) if kind == "batch"
+                    else _together(wt, branch, head, root, on, under))
+        if not partners:
